# Copy to .env for local development, or set these as real environment
# variables in production. Never commit a real .env file (see .gitignore) —
# this file contains no real values, only names and safe example defaults.
# Full explanation of every variable: /docs/DEPLOYMENT.md "Environment variables".

# --- Shared by the API and worker processes -------------------------------

# Path to the SQLite database file. Both processes MUST point at the same
# file. The parent directory is created automatically if it doesn't exist.
DB_PATH=./.data/platform.sqlite

# Root directory for per-task ephemeral git workspaces. Both processes MUST
# point at the same directory.
WORKSPACES_ROOT=./.data/workspaces

# Root directory for persistent, project-scoped ProjectWorkspace directories (Phase 3 — see
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md). Completely separate from WORKSPACES_ROOT above: a ProjectWorkspace
# directory is created once alongside its project and never torn down by task execution, unlike the
# ephemeral per-task workspaces under WORKSPACES_ROOT. Both processes MUST point at the same
# directory.
PROJECT_WORKSPACE_ROOT=./.data/project-workspaces

# Which CodingEngine drives task execution: opencode | claude-code | fixture
# `fixture` is deterministic and makes no live model calls — local dev/CI
# only. Refused at startup if set together with NODE_ENV=production.
CODING_ENGINE=opencode

# development | test | production. Controls the session cookie's `Secure`
# flag (AUTHENTICATION.md), the CODING_ENGINE=fixture guard above, and the
# ALLOW_LOCAL_GIT_PROJECTS guard below. Unset (or anything other than
# "production") behaves as development.
NODE_ENV=development

# Only meaningful when NODE_ENV=production. local-git projects (no GitHub
# account, no Pull Request, no merge signal — see docs/GITHUB_INTEGRATION.md
# "Local git / plain-git testing") are disabled in production unless this is
# explicitly "true". Ignored (local-git always allowed) outside production.
# Must be exactly "true" or "false" if set — anything else fails startup.
# ALLOW_LOCAL_GIT_PROJECTS=false

# --- API process (src/api/server.ts) ---------------------------------------

# TCP port the API listens on.
PORT=3000

# --- Worker process (src/worker/run-worker.ts) ------------------------------

# How often (ms) the worker polls for claimable TODO/WORKING/REVIEW tasks.
POLL_INTERVAL_MS=2000

# Upper bound on how many TODO/WORKING task executions this worker process runs at once (see
# docs/TASK_LIFECYCLE.md "Worker concurrency and lease loss"). A conservative default — each execution can spawn a live
# coding-engine child process and a full git workspace clone, so this bounds real resource usage,
# not just a counter. Tasks beyond capacity simply stay TODO and are picked up on a later poll —
# there is no queue and no separate "blocked" state. Does NOT bound the separate, cheap
# REVIEW/human-review-feedback polling path. Must be a positive integer if set.
WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENT_TASKS=2

# Backlog analysis (Phase 4A.1) runs in the WORKER process, never in an API request. This is the
# GLOBAL cap on analyses running at the same time across ALL worker processes sharing this database
# (each is one real model call) — it is enforced by the database claim, so 3 workers with cap 2 still
# means at most 2 live analyses in total, not 6. Positive integer, at most 16. Default 2.
# See docs/BACKLOG_INTELLIGENCE.md "Global concurrency cap".
MAX_BACKLOG_ANALYSIS_CONCURRENCY=2

# How often (ms) a real GitHub call is allowed to happen for a single REVIEW task's PR — a
# SEPARATE, different cadence from POLL_INTERVAL_MS above. POLL_INTERVAL_MS still governs how often
# the worker's tick() runs at all (checking for due TODO/WORKING/REVIEW work, cheap indexed SQL
# queries only); REVIEW_POLL_INTERVAL_MS governs how often, once a REVIEW task is looked at, a real
# GitHub call (PR status + human-feedback check) is actually made for it. A REVIEW task's schedule
# is tracked per-task in the database (tasks.next_review_check_at) and claimed atomically
# (TaskRepository.claimReviewCheck), so every worker process sharing this database — not just this
# one — is bounded by the same schedule; increasing worker process count never increases GitHub call
# volume per REVIEW task. See docs/TASK_LIFECYCLE.md "REVIEW polling" and
# migrations/010_review_poll_scheduling.sql. Must be a positive integer if set.
REVIEW_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=30000

# Identifies this worker process's lease ownership (TASK_LIFECYCLE.md "Crash
# recovery"). Defaults to a random id per process if unset — set explicitly
# only if you want stable, recognizable ids across restarts in logs/metrics.
# IMPORTANT (multiple workers): every worker process MUST have a DISTINCT id. The claim guard lets a
# task's current owner id re-claim it, so two processes sharing one WORKER_ID could both execute the
# same task. Leave it unset unless you run exactly one worker per id.
# WORKER_ID=worker-1

# How long (ms) a worker's claim on a task is valid before another worker may
# reclaim it as crashed (TASK_LIFECYCLE.md "Crash recovery"). Defaults to
# 300000 (5 minutes) — deliberately left unset in real deployments. Only
# useful for shortening lease-expiry/crash-recovery to observable timescales
# during manual local testing; setting this must go together with
# LEASE_HEARTBEAT_MS (kept well below it) or a genuinely still-running task
# will falsely appear crashed and be reclaimed mid-execution.
# TASK_LEASE_MS=300000

# How often (ms) a live execution renews its own lease while still running —
# must stay well below TASK_LEASE_MS. Defaults to 60000 (1 minute). Same
# manual-testing-only caveat as TASK_LEASE_MS above.
# LEASE_HEARTBEAT_MS=60000

# --- Infrastructure retry (V1.1 Slice 4) — defaults shown; rarely need changing ---
# Extra whole-episode attempts after a transient infra failure (provider timeout/quota, git clone,
# GitHub 5xx) before a task is marked FAILED; extra in-place retries for a check that could not run;
# and the bounded backoff between them. Positive integers (ms for the delays).
# INFRA_MAX_EPISODE_RETRIES=2
# INFRA_MAX_CHECK_RETRIES=2
# INFRA_RETRY_BASE_DELAY_MS=2000
# INFRA_RETRY_MAX_DELAY_MS=30000

# --- HTTP / deployment (Developer Alpha — see docs/INTERNAL_ALPHA.md) --------
# The API and web UI MUST share one origin (the Vite dev proxy locally, Caddy in deploy/). There is no
# CORS configuration: a browser on a different origin is refused by design (SameSite=Lax session).
#
# Force the session cookie's Secure flag on/off independent of NODE_ENV. Unset = Secure iff
# NODE_ENV=production. Set COOKIE_SECURE=false ONLY for an alpha box served over plain HTTP (a Secure
# cookie is silently dropped by browsers on http://); set it true behind TLS. Must be true|false.
# COOKIE_SECURE=false
# Number of reverse-proxy hops to trust so the login rate limiter sees the real client IP (1 behind
# Caddy). Unset = don't trust proxy headers. Must be a non-negative integer.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# --- INTERNAL ALPHA (NODE_ENV=production) checklist -------------------------------
# With NODE_ENV=production the API and worker REFUSE to start unless ALL of these are set: DB_PATH,
# WORKSPACES_ROOT and PROJECT_WORKSPACE_ROOT (ABSOLUTE paths, identical for both processes),
# CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY (back it up separately from the database), and CODING_ENGINE must not be
# `fixture`. Also set OPENCODE_MODEL and the matching provider API key below, and give every worker a
# distinct (or unset) WORKER_ID. Development (NODE_ENV unset/development) needs none of these.

# --- GitHub integration (only required if any project uses source-control
# -provider "github" — see docs/GITHUB_INTEGRATION.md) ----------------------

# Base64-encoded 32-byte key used to encrypt stored GitHub tokens at rest
# (AES-256-GCM). Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# Validated (format only, never logged) at process startup if set.
# CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- OpenCode coding engine (only used when CODING_ENGINE=opencode) --------

# Path to the opencode binary, if not on PATH.
# OPENCODE_BIN_PATH=opencode

# Model passed to opencode via --model. If unset, opencode's own default is
# used — see docs/CODING_ENGINE.md "Configuration".
# OPENCODE_MODEL=

# Retry/backoff tuning for transient provider failures (503/429/etc.) — see
# docs/CODING_ENGINE.md "Retry behavior". Defaults shown; rarely need changing.
# OPENCODE_MAX_RETRIES=3
# OPENCODE_RETRY_BASE_DELAY_MS=1000
# OPENCODE_RETRY_MAX_DELAY_MS=20000

# The opencode CLI reads its own model-provider API key directly from the
# process environment — this platform's code never reads or forwards it
# except to pass the environment through (see docs/CODING_ENGINE.md
# "Security & Environment Sanitization"). Set whichever one your chosen
# model provider needs, e.g.:
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
